"Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual. - ORA - Oxford University Research Archive (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3Aad6962cc-e146-404c-a424-84e7bb2afc95)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.8 years ago; Crawled: today; ## Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual. ... Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual.\n\nImage: Journal article icon\n\n# Journal article\n\n## Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual.\n\nAbstract:\n    Limits of agreement provide a straightforward and intuitive approach to agreement between different methods for measuring the same quantity. When pairs of observations using the two methods are independent, i.e., on different subjects, the calculations are very simple and straightforward. Some authors collect repeated data, either as repeated pairs of measurements on the same subject, whose true value of the measured quantity may be changing, or more than one measurement by one or both methods of an unchanging underlying quantity. In this paper we describe methods for analysing such clustered observations, both when the underlying quantity is assumed to be changing and when it is not.\n\nPublication status:\n    Published\n\n## APA Style\n\nBland, J., & Altman, D. (2007). Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual. Journal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics, 17(4), 571–582.\n\nCopy APA Style [Input]\n\n## MLA Style\n\nBland, J., and D. Altman. “Agreement between Methods of Measurement with Multiple Observations per Individual.” Journal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics, vol. 17, no. 4, 2007, pp. 571–82.\n\nCopy MLA Style [Input]\n\n## Chicago Style\n\nBland, J., and D. Altman. 2007. “Agreement between Methods of Measurement with Multiple Observations per Individual.” Journal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics 17 (4): 571–82.\n\nCopy Chicago Style [Input]\n\nPublisher copy:\n    10.1080/10543400701329422\n\n### Authors\n\n* * *\n\n[Input] [Input]\n\n+ Bland, J More by this author\n\nRole:\n    Author\n\n+ Altman, D More by this author\n\nRole:\n    Author\n\n### Bibliographic Details\n\n* * *\n\nJournal:\n    Journal of biopharmaceutical statistics More from this journal\nVolume:\n    17\nIssue:\n    4\nPages:\n    571-582\nPublication date:\n    2007-01-01\nDOI:\n    \n\n10.1080/10543400701329422\n\nEISSN:\n    \n\n1520-5711\n\nISSN:\n    \n\n1054-3406\n\nLanguage:\n    \n\nEnglish\n\nKeywords:\n    \n\nHumans\n\nAnalysis of Variance\n\nDiagnostic Techniques and Procedures\n\nAlgorithms\n\nReproducibility of Results\n\nRadionuclide Ventriculography\n\nCardiography, Impedance\n\nBiometry\n\nClinical Laboratory Techniques\n\nStroke Volume\n\nPubs id:\n    \n\npubs:317974\n\nUUID:\n    \n\nuuid:ad6962cc-e146-404c-a424-84e7bb2afc95\n\nLocal pid:\n    \n\npubs:317974\n\nSource identifiers:\n    \n\n317974\n\nDeposit date:\n    \n\n2013-11-16\n\nARK identifier:\n    \n\nark:/29072/ora_ad6962cce146404ca42484e7bb2afc95\n\nCopyright date:\n    2007\n\nLicence:\n    Terms and Conditions of Use for Oxford University Research Archive--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBland-Altman plots when you have collected multiple observations per individual - FAQ 1521 - GraphPad (https://www.graphpad.com/support/faq/bland-altman-plots-when-you-have-collected-multiple-observations-per-individual/)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Bland and Altman have published extensions of their method to deal with this situation, but Prism does not (yet) implement this method. ... Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual.\n\nKNOWLEDGEBASE - ARTICLE #1521\n\n# Bland-Altman plots when you have collected multiple observations per individual\n\n The method that Prism uses to create Bland-Altman plots implicitly assumes that each pair of values entered is independent. This assumption is violated when you collect data from multiple individuals and measure each individual multiple times. If you have ten individuals, and use both methods three times on each, you do not have thirty independent pairs of measurements. If you enter all the data into Prism, the resulting Bland-Altman plot will be misleading. \n\nBland and Altman have published extensions of their method to deal with this situation, but Prism does not (yet) implement this method.\n\n Bland and Altman. Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual. Journal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics (2007) vol. 17 pp. 571-582--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgreement Between Methods of Measurement with Multiple Observations Per Individual (https://scispace.com/pdf/agreement-between-methods-of-measurement-with-multiple-4pp29ouprk.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Agreement Between Methods of Measurement with Multiple Observations Per Individual ... Martin and Altman, Douglas G. , (2007) 'Agreement Between Methods of Measurement with Multiple Observations Per Individual', Journal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics, 17:4, 571 - 582\nThis article was downloaded by:[Bland, J. Martin]  \n[Bland, J. Martin]\n\nOn: 29 June 2007  \nAccess Details: [subscription number 780214976]  \nPublisher: Taylor & Francis  \nInforma Ltd Registered in England and Wales Registered Number: 1072954  \nRegistered office: Mortimer House, 37-41 Mortimer Street, London W1T 3JH, UK\n\nJournal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics\n\nPublication details, including instructions for authors and subscription information:  \nhttp://www.informaworld.com/smpp/title~content=t713597263\n\nAgreement Between Methods of Measurement with Multiple Observations Per Individual\n\nOnline Publication Date: 01 July 2007  \nTo cite this Article: Bland, J. Martin and Altman, Douglas 