d-Altman plots should use X, not (Y+X)/2 when X is a reference method. Statistics in Medicine 27:778-780.\n  * Zou GY (2013) Confidence interval estimation for the Bland-Altman limits of agreement with multiple observations per individual. Statistical Methods in Medical Research 22:630-642.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA scoping review of statistical methods for the analysis of method comparison studies with repeated measurements of clinical data | BMC Medical Research Methodology | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12874-026-02824-1)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; This scoping review synthesizes existing statistical approaches for the analysis of method comparison studies with repeated measurements of clinical data and summarizes how these methods perform relative to different versions of the limits-of-agreement approach. ... Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual.\n## Conclusion\n\nThis scoping review synthesizes existing statistical approaches for the analysis of method comparison studies with repeated measurements of clinical data and summarizes how these methods perform relative to different versions of the limits-of-agreement approach. Overall, the findings suggest that adjusted limits of agreement are often more appropriate for repeated-measurement designs, as they account for within-subject variability while preserving an absolute interpretation of agreement.\n\n## Data availability\n\nAll data are presented in the main manuscript or additional supporting files.\n\n\n## References\n\n  1. Bland JM, Altman DG. Measuring agreement in method comparison studies. Stat Methods Med Res. 1999;8:135–60.\n\n  2. Bland JM, Altman DG. Agreement between methods of measurement with multiple observations per individual. J Biopharm Stat. 2007;17(4):571–82. https://doi.org/10.1080/10543400701329422.\